WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.180
ANDY CHANG: Welcome to
SCC 208, Shared Fate.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.940
In this talk, you'll learn
how Google Cloud is working

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.790
to enable you to move from
a shared responsibility

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.740
model for security
and public cloud

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.700
to a shared fate model, where
we as the cloud provider

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.370
collaborate much more
strongly with you

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:31.430
and take additional
responsibility for making

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.190
sure what's deployed
together is in the most

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.230
secure and compliant state.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.860
A core component of
this ability to change

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.680
how risk is transferred
is providing you

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.780
with production-proven
blueprints

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.070
for foundational infrastructure,
workloads, and applications

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.320
built with Google Cloud
best practices and patterns

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.950
so that collectively we
have a much better view

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.350
and understanding of
the quantified risk

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.120
and the security
posture being deployed.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.830
I'm Andy Chang, senior product
manager for the Cloud Security

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:04.330
team.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.610
As we've seen with
many customers,

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.510
an ongoing desire is to
get started and operate

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.960
much quicker but with
also additional security

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.290
so that the value of Cloud
can reflect in the businesses

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.580
that you run.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.970
The approach we're now taking
to enable and accelerate this

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.870
is building out a set of
opinionated security foundation

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.780
and posture blueprints to
automatically allow you

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.590
to leverage predefined Google
Cloud security best practices

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.050
for your infrastructure
and for your workloads.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.440
This is part of the
overall Google Cloud

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.550
vision in security for going
even further in our partnership

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.730
with our customers.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:52.200
We want to move the industry
and help move the industry

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.690
from current shared
responsibility

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.780
approach to a shared fate
model, where we're truly

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.100
partners in your success
and in achieving your goals.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.760
And this won't happen overnight.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.420
But the key pieces
are coming into place.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.419
First, a necessary
condition for this

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.000
is that the underlying
Cloud infrastructure

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.510
is highly trusted and secure,
that we're providing you

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.720
with that secure
compliant platform as well

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.540
as a portfolio of security
and compliance products

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.550
and solutions that you can use.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.370
The good news is that
our mission to make

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.930
Google the most trusted cloud--

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.650
we're well along the way there.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.880
Next, we're providing customers
with opinionated foundational

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.880
and posture blueprints matched
to the type of workloads

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.010
that they need, which
enable you, again,

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.590
to build on top of the
underlying services,

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.740
to stack and build posture
blueprints on top of landing

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.700
zones, and which all have
Google best practices in terms

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.700
of configuration,
architecture, security posture

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.260
embed with them.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.690
You're therefore able to stack
these opinionated posture

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.940
blueprints on top
of landing zones

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.730
and take advantage
of the knowledge

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.770
that Google's already
built around security.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.590
We then take the next step
and think about taking

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.980
the blueprints as the basis for
end-to-end solutions to solve

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.640
very specific
business problems--

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.510
for example, web application
API protections, zero trust,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.120
and BeyondCorp enterprise
solutions and security

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.640
analytics at scale.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.610
By combining these
together, you're

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:30.060
now able to get the best
practices in a deployable form

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.930
targeted towards solving very
specific business problems.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.040
Finally, by having
the combination

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.230
of the blueprints
and the solutions,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.320
it leads to a significant
increase in the ability for you

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.350
to use consistent approved
end-to-end patterns

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.260
for complete business
processes and applications.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.620
That consistency
and greater default

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.650
secure configuration and
best practice adoption

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.340
leads to a much more
quantified risk model,

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.370
allows us and you to better
understand the architecture

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.310
and risks of being deployed
in your infrastructure

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.960
and your workloads,
and therefore

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.810
allows us to take on more
responsibility collaborating

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.050
with you and enabling different
models of risk transfer.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.170
As we launched both our risk
manager and our risk protection

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.410
program, this allows you, if
you've adopted these patterns,

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.079
to take advantage of some
specific cyber insurance

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.960
offerings, which
will allow you to now

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.290
have more options for that risk
transfer and risk management.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.020
Along the evolution
path of shared fate,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.800
in addition to changing how you
as customers can manage risk,

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.440
we see parallel
transformation opportunities

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.420
in other key tracks as well.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.160
Today many customers
in public cloud

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.670
have complex experiences
for security and governance.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.830
And we see an
opportunity for use

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.290
the jobs to be done
framework to change these

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.550
into much more consistent and
complete end-to-end journeys.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.320
Similarly, many
customers in public cloud

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.950
have disjoint experiences
managing policy and governance

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:05.010
for both their individual
instances in public cloud,

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.800
as well as across public
cloud and on prem.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.050
We feel, again, that
there is an opportunity

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.070
to change this fundamentally
into a much more coordinated

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.800
and verifiable experience.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.550
Blueprints play a key
role both in moving

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.270
product security from a
bolted on after effect

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.300
to a built in part of
the underlying solution,

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.820
and also from moving
platform security

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.280
from a set of complex
configurations

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.220
to increasingly
secure by default.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:40.760
As we think about the stack
from a customer perspective,

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.980
as we talked about,
we first build

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.370
on top of that secure core
Google infrastructure.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.770
And with the next layer
of security products

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.730
like SCC Premium, Assured
Workloads, Organization Policy,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:56.750
VPC Service Controls giving
you additional capabilities

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.540
differentiated in their
ability to help you manage

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.700
your security posture,
your visibility,

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.260
and your segmentation.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.810
These form the lowest layer
of the security stack.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.330
On top of this
baseline comes both

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.180
the foundational and
posture blueprints,

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.560
where the foundational
blueprints

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.950
include org policy, resource
hierarchy, networking,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.240
access segmentation,
logging, detective controls

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.630
to allow you to take
advantage of proven designs

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.850
to build your foundational
infrastructure.

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:28.040
And on top of that,
the posture blueprints

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.580
cover specific sets of
services aggregated together.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.000
And both the individual
service specific controls

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.400
as well as the
intraservices controls

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:41.030
necessary for the
workloads to work properly.

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:43.010
The blueprints come
in three tiers.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.070
And they differ really in
their level of native support

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.710
and their lifecycle management.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:51.230
The three tiers are
supported blueprints,

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:54.530
verified blueprints, and
compatible blueprints.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:57.470
Supported blueprints are
created by Google security teams

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:01.940
and managed as products with
regular six month refreshes

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.340
and attestation and testing
of the functionality

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.760
and resilience, as well as
the interoperability testing

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.710
between them.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:12.740
Verified blueprints can be
created by Google teams,

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.930
partner teams, or customers.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:18.140
The key is that the lifecycle
management, as well as

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:21.110
a station fit for purpose,
are the responsibility

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.880
of the creating team.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.950
Compatible blueprints
are just that.

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:25.947
They're compatible.

00:07:25.947 --> 00:07:27.530
I mean, they can be
created by anyone.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.470
And their requirement is that
they need to be from a format

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.890
and also a style
standpoint correctly

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:38.000
matched to be operated with our
blueprint tools and a blueprint

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.550
engine.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.400
And finally, on top of
the posture blueprints,

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.620
we have security solutions.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.230
And these solutions
address specific use cases

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:51.850
and further add the wrappings
of go to market collateral, best

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:55.840
practice, application notes
so that you can adopt these

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:57.220
for your particular use cases.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:07.700
We focus today really on the
security foundations blueprints

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.280
as well as the security
posture blueprints

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:13.840
and their relationship
to each other.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.220
We think about the core
foundational blueprint.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.130
The key is to allow
you to start up front

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.410
with best practices embedded in.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:22.850
It creates a better
starting point

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:25.850
as well for security and
compliance, as the security

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.520
best practices encoded in
our foundation blueprint

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.250
give you a great
start to be compatible

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:35.299
and meeting certain requirements
within CIS, PCI-DSS,

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:41.350
NIST-800-53, as
well as ISO27001.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:45.280
In addition, they allow you
to implement key security

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:47.710
best practices and
philosophies core

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.860
to how Google protects itself.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.680
These key principles really
fall into three categories--

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:58.420
first that all defense is
always executed at in depth

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.420
and at scale so multiple
layers of protection

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:05.830
separate something of interest
from potential external threat.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:09.100
They allow you to adopt
the BeyondProd model

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:14.530
for deployment centric
infrastructure and the ability

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:16.600
to have a zero trust
model around both

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.310
the infrastructure
you build as well

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.020
as the applications you build.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:23.410
And then also, they de-risk the
cloud deployment and adoption

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:26.980
by moving you to these
proven patterns that

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:30.430
allow you to move towards a
more shared fate relationship

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:31.780
between yourselves and Google.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:36.520
To dive in a little
bit more detail

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.470
on the security
foundations blueprint,

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.020
it provides that
strong starting point

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.490
we talked about in baseline.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.770
It's delivered not
only in documentation

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.530
but infrastructure as code
in deployable terraform

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:51.190
that automates, accelerates
your ability to use it.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:53.230
It encodes those best
practices and can

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:56.170
be deployed either at an
organization level or a folder

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.010
level.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.590
And it has a six-month
lifecycle refresh.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:02.890
We deployed the very first
version back in August 2020.

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:06.560
The second version was
deployed in April of this year.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:11.450
And then these three will
be launching in October.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:14.420
And the posture blueprints that
we'll talk about build on top

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:18.080
of it are tested and
verified against it.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.920
One example of the
security posture blueprint

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.450
is the one we're building
for secured data warehousing.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.960
Secure data analytics
and data warehousing

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.230
are a key reason many customers
come to Google, as we're

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:35.320
differentiated in our BigQuery
product and the things

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.240
we do in data analytics.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.790
As you can see here,
we've adopted architecture

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:42.550
that has the landing
area and ingest,

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:45.250
the data warehouse itself,
the storage and processing,

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:47.500
classification and
governance pieces,

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:51.190
as well as security, posture,
and detection for threats.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:54.160
In addition, there's also
the optional presentation

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:56.890
layer, which in the case of
the blueprints we've built,

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:00.940
we provide an example in
the form of an AI notebook.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:03.190
To dive this a little
bit more detail, what

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:05.500
you see in the next level of
detail of the architecture

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:09.010
is we've segmented this
into four different projects

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:12.070
within the BigQuery itself and
then separate set of projects

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.080
for the AI Notebook.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:17.560
These projects not only
create resource separation,

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:19.630
but are also their
own BPC service

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.030
perimeters, which
allow us to manage

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:25.720
both sensitive and
non-sensitive data.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:28.600
The ingest contains
the ability to ingest

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.348
both batch and streaming data
and then de-identification

00:11:32.348 --> 00:11:34.390
of all the data so it can
be correctly classified

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:37.780
into the non-sensitive and
sensitive areas of BigQuery.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.950
We manage the IM structures,
the data encryption, and also

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.860
data classification so
that using Data Catalog you

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:48.670
can have column level access
control of the BigQuery Data

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.180
Warehouse.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:53.728
And this architecture is based
on proven production instances

00:11:53.728 --> 00:11:56.020
we've built together with
some of our financial service

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.830
customers so that we have good
confidence that the scales

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:04.430
and works well for those
regulated industries.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.050
We dive into a little bit more--

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:10.880
what is in the BigQuery Data
Warehouse blueprint in addition

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.730
to the terraform itself
and deployable instances,

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:18.020
it has a set of documentation
that comes with it around--

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:21.140
accessible through either the
architecture center or the best

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:24.920
practice site, quick starts,
technical design docs,

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:29.210
solution guides, the code itself
with the guide for deployment

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.460
of the code-- terraform code--

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:36.020
overview decks, licensing and
pricing models, public talks,

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:39.440
the blogs, and also application
notes for specific use cases

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:42.440
as examples to get you
started more quickly.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.750
It too is on a
six-month refresh cycle,

00:12:44.750 --> 00:12:47.630
with the first version just
being launched here in October

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:54.090
and then the V2 targeted
for a Q2 2022 launch.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:57.348
Built on top and also available
as a standalone-- what

00:12:57.348 --> 00:12:59.640
this means is that with each
of the posture blueprints,

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.680
they are first and
foremost able to be

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:03.990
compatible with our security
foundations blueprint

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:06.420
and tested from an
integration standpoint

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.370
with that and the other
posture blueprints.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.650
But also, they're
deployable in standalone

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:13.170
in your own Brownfield.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.450
And then they include
third party attestations

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:18.750
around the security architecture
design, the stride threat

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.880
modeling as well as
being pen tested.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.410
And you'll see
what this blueprint

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:26.520
in Q4 being tested against
NIST-800-53 compliance

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.640
standard in detail.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:31.970
When we think about the
controls that are fundamentally

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.010
built into the BigQuery
Data Warehouse,

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:36.150
they fall in three categories.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:39.350
Controls that manage
configuration state--

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:42.770
as misconfiguration is one of
the largest sources of data

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:44.865
breaches, we
particularly provide

00:13:44.865 --> 00:13:47.150
and code best practices
for configuration

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:50.810
of each of the services as well
as integration and interaction

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:54.110
of the services within the
BigQuery secure data warehouse

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:54.980
blueprint.

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.260
Behavioral controls
focuse specifically

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:59.690
around the design and
architecture of the secure data

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:02.210
warehouse to look
at specific threats

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:06.920
from either data exfiltration,
from privilege escalation,

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:09.830
from things that break
the segmentation model,

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:12.890
the built in, and then
environmental controls.

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:17.000
As we talked about before, the
secure data warehouse blueprint

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.970
can be deployed either as
a standalone, in which case

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:22.160
the environmental
controls are fully encased

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:23.780
with that particular blueprint.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:27.110
Or if they are deployed on
top of a security foundations

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:30.290
blueprint, they inherit
the environmental controls

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:34.170
from the underlying
core infrastructure.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:35.740
We're now going
to move to a demo.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.880
We're going to walk you through
a fully deployed secure data

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.480
warehouse and highlight
some of the configuration

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:47.290
controls, the behavioral
security protections,

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:50.640
as well as the
environmental controls.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:53.040
Here we have a demo
organization in which

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.200
we've deployed the
secured data warehouse

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.680
blueprint in its own folder.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.240
The secured data
warehouse blueprint

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:03.330
consists of four
distinct projects--

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:07.650
a data ingestion project,
which includes Cloud Storage,

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:12.180
Pub/Sub, and Dataflow, a
data lake project, which

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:17.510
includes the BigQuery instance
for non-sensitive data,

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:19.730
privileged BigQuery
Data Warehouse,

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:23.420
which includes Cloud Storage as
well as the privilege BigQuery

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:28.220
Data Warehouse instance, and
a data governance project,

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:34.370
which includes Data Catalog,
key management, and data

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:37.740
classification through DLP.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.980
When deploying these
into your own Brownfield

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.140
or on top of the
foundations blueprint,

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:46.290
you can plug in the
data encryption keys

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:49.110
into your existing
encryption key project,

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:51.450
logging into your
centralized lodging project,

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:55.110
and the network connections
to allow the Dataflow

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:58.240
templates in through
your existing system.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.740
You can also run this standalone
if you want to experiment

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:02.650
and play with it.

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:05.730
And to do that, we include
two harness projects, which

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:10.530
supply the Dataflow template
and the terraform sourcing.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:16.530
The security perimeters around
the BigQuery Data Warehouse

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.420
and the other projects
are set up as follows.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:21.270
We have three
separate perimeters--

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:24.600
one around ingestion, one
around the privileged data,

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.940
and then one around
data governance.

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.570
In addition, we set up
bridges between ingestion

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:33.630
and governance, privilege
and governance, and then

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.070
privilege and ingestion.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:43.420
If we look at the VPC
Service perimeter around data

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:43.920
ingestion.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.620
You'll see it includes the
ingestion project and the data

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.470
lake, has its own
defined access context

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:53.770
levels, and specific
egress rules.

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:56.560
Similarly, if we look at
the VPC Service perimeter

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.170
around privileged
data, it captures

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:03.220
the privileged BigQuery
Data Warehouse project

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:07.750
as its own access levels
defined at egress rules.

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:10.160
And then the last perimeter
is around data governance,

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:14.700
which secures the data
governance project

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:18.630
and has its own
access levels defined.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:21.390
If we look now at the
bridging, the first bridge

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:26.640
connects the ingestion, the
data lake and governance.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:32.440
The second bridge connects the
data lake to the privileged.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:35.700
And then the final bridge
connects the data governance

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:38.760
to privileged.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.940
As you can see, we've
defined access policies

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:46.560
for each of the separate
VPC Service perimeters.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:51.750
We've also used our DLP to
de-identify specifically card

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:56.670
number and card pin data so that
in the non-sensitive BigQuery

00:17:56.670 --> 00:18:01.680
instance, they can be used by
lower access levels and folks

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:03.870
with more generalized roles--

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.990
separate from when there
are the actual values

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:12.730
are being used in the
privileged data warehouse.

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.910
We use Data Catalog
with policy tags

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:21.250
in order to further provide
column level control.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:25.810
You can see we use
column level policy

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:29.020
tags for person name,
credit limit, and also

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:30.370
credit card number.

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:33.710
So we give you additional
fine grained control of who,

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:35.500
what service
accounts, what humans

00:18:35.500 --> 00:18:38.215
can access these pieces of
sensitive data as an example.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:44.580
If we now look at
the data lake, which

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:47.070
has the non-sensitive
data set, you

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:54.810
can see that if we look at,
again, card number and card pin

00:18:54.810 --> 00:19:04.740
and create a query, you
see de-identified version

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:07.500
of both card pin
and card number that

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:11.240
is still format preserving.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:15.720
If we now move to the sensitive
data and the privileged data

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:16.960
warehouse--

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:20.400
if we were to look at
the card pin data--

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:27.810
because I have the
right set of accesses,

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:30.000
you'll see the
actual card pin data

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.510
within the privileged and
protected BigQuery instance.

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:39.530
However, to show you that we're
using column level control,

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:45.210
I'm now also going to try
to query the card number.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:47.280
And here you see
access has been denied

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:49.020
because this particular
user currently

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:55.430
does not have the IM permissions
to access the card numbers.

00:19:55.430 --> 00:20:03.240
We can fix this by going back
and granting the permissions

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.510
for fine grained reader
on that particular column.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:29.760
And now if we rerun
the query, we'll

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:34.750
see we get both the pin and
the underlying card numbers.

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:38.160
So we've now shown you
the architecture example

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:42.330
for the secure data warehouse
containing three VPC Service

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:46.980
parameters and both data
lake for non-sensitive data

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.070
and a separate secure
data set, which

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:54.390
has column level access managed
through Data Catalog policy

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:56.550
text.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:59.730
We've now shown you a deployed
version of the secure data

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:00.870
warehouse blueprint.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:03.570
And we walked you through
some of the key configuration,

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:06.240
behavioral, and
environmental controls.

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:07.890
So hopefully this
provides you a view

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:11.070
of how we're embedding
best practices in security

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:13.380
as well as the
architecture to give you

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.680
something as a reference,
starting point, and pattern

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:19.590
to jump-start your workloads
and production around data

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:22.660
warehousing and data analytics.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.210
To learn more,
you can explore at

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:29.140
cloud.google.com/security/best
practices the range

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:32.470
of our current portfolio of
blueprints for foundation

00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:35.560
and for security posture,
explore the documentation

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.320
as well as the terraform
repos that we've supplied,

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:40.510
and then download kind
of reference guides from

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:42.400
a step-by-step basis.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.800
We'd highly encourage you
to deploy these security

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.320
blueprints into your own
example, organizations,

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:50.710
and folders so that
you can experiment

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:53.110
to see how they fit
with your environments

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:55.930
and definitely encourage
you to customize and use

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:58.150
the module as a
script if they create

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:01.360
additional differentiation
for your particular needs.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:03.400
As we talked about,
these blueprints

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:05.080
are refreshed on
a six-month cycle.

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:07.570
So we highly encourage you
to also feed back to us

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:09.790
where you've seen gaps
and things that you'd want

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:11.330
to see in future iterations.

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:14.350
We can put that in the
backlog and many of your needs

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:16.870
may show up in future
versions of the blueprint

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:19.120
so you can use a standard
blueprint as opposed

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:21.672
to one that you've
[INAUDIBLE] yourself.

00:22:21.672 --> 00:22:24.130
And lastly, we encourage you
to start trialing the Security

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:27.370
Command Center Premium, as
it provides an overall Cloud

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:29.230
Security posture
management around not only

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:33.010
your organization but
these multi-project and

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:35.350
folder-centric posture
blueprints as well.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:40.830
Thank you so much for your
time and the opportunity

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:42.660
to share with you what
we're thinking about

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.000
on the shared faith
standpoint as well as some

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:48.060
of those core blueprints that
allow you to jump-start and get

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:50.940
started with the best
practices and patterns

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.110
that can help you
be more successful.

